# General Rules

- Assume I am on a UNIX-like OS
- Concise and succinct answers are my preferred response
- Ideas are tracked in `~/.claude/ideas/*.md` — managed via `/proj-ideate`
- Active projects live in `~/.claude/ideas/projects/` — kicked off via `/proj-kickoff`

# Writing Code

- Test first approach is required. Write tests first, then implement against them.
- Agentic flow is required. If no agents exist, spin them up.
- Agents should spin up other agents to do implementation or code reviews

# Ownership

- You have ownership of anything in the local environment
- You have no write access in production.
- If the user asks for queries or code to run in production, that is fine. The user will own execution.

# Git

- Prefer using git worktrees for isolated work, especially when multiple Claudes are working in parallel.
- Create worktrees under `~/.claude/worktrees/`, NOT under the project's `.claude/` directory. Keep the project's `.claude` dir clean.
- Branch names must be prefixed with `bryan/` (e.g. `bryan/enterprise-entitlements`).
- Example: `git worktree add ~/.claude/worktrees/<name> -b bryan/<name> <base-branch>`
- Never commit. The user likes to own comitting

# Project Management

- Ideas are categorized in `~/.claude/ideas/<category>.md` (e.g. work.md, personal.md)
- Each idea has an ID (e.g. WORK-1) and priority (P0-P4)
- Active projects are kicked off from ideas via `/proj-kickoff` and live in `~/.claude/ideas/projects/`

# Planning

- The user is a heavy claude code user and manages projects and tasks from ~/.claude
- Repos that leverage claude will have a .claude folder. If it doesn't exist, assume the user doesn't want claude in there

## Problem Statement and Solution

- Projects have a problem statement. This cannot be edited by anyone but the user.
- Projects have a proposed solution. This cannot be edited by anyone but the user.

### Planning Work
- Once the user approves a plan, tasks should be broken down under the project in the .md or .org file
- Link the claude plan.md file to the project in the .md or .org file
- Ask the user to approve the work breakdown before moving forward. Apply any feedback.

#### Tasks v Subtasks

- Tasks and subtasks always require an agent team
- Tasks and subtasks can be modified by anyone
- Tasks and subtasks should always be done in plan mode
- Solution can only be deemed "shipped" by the user but tasks and subtasks can be marked as complete by the agent
- Tasks and subtasks can be worked on in parallel since we leverage worktrees
- Small, focused tasks are better than a task that says "implement feature"

### Project Priorities

- Projects use a PN priority system
  - P0: Critical
  - P1: High
  - P2: Important
  - P3: Low
  - P4: Nice to have
- P0 can be implemented at any time
- P1 can be implemented at any time after P0s are done
- P2 or lower need user approval

## Markdown

- Projects are listed in priority order (P0 -> P4)
- Tasks should use `[ ]` syntax

## Org Mode

- Agents should leverage org mode's priorities, deadlines, schedules, and task management.
- Agents must set org priorities (`[#A]`, `[#B]`, `[#C]`) on tasks and subtasks when creating them
- Use tags for filtering and status (e.g. `:backend:`, `:frontend:`, `:blocked:`, `:waiting:`)
- Use property drawers for structured metadata (linked plan file, owning agent/worktree, base branch)
- Use `SCHEDULED` and `DEADLINE` when dates are known
- Do NOT use: clocking (`CLOCK:`), effort estimates, babel/code blocks, custom link types, or column view — these add noise with no value for agents
- All org file reads/writes must go through the `org-bot` agent — other agents should not directly modify org files
- Use `[/]` (or `[%]`) statistics cookies on parent headings to track task/subtask completion (e.g. `** TODO Implement auth [/]`)
- Archive completed tasks with `:ARCHIVE:` tag or move to `+archive.org` sibling file to keep active files clean
- Refile tasks to the appropriate heading/file when scope or ownership changes — don't leave orphaned TODOs
- When a project is officially kicked off, move it from the category file (e.g. `work.org`) to `./projects/PROJECT-NAME.org` as a dedicated project file

# Agentic Workflow

## Ideation
- Ideas live in `~/.claude/ideas/*.md` with IDs (e.g. WORK-1, PERSONAL-3)
- `/proj-ideate <category> <priority>: <description>` — add an idea
- `/proj-ideate list` — list all ideas sorted P0→P4, alpha within each
- .md files are brainstorming — no commitment to implement

## Initializing a project
- `/proj-init <ID>` (e.g. `/proj-init WORK-3`) — create a project file from an idea with sections to fill out
- Creates `~/.claude/ideas/projects/<id>-<slug>.md` (e.g. `work-5-migrate-existing-one-time-gifts.md`) with Context, Problem Statement, Solution, and Technical Requirements sections
- Project files must include the task ID prefix in the filename for easy lookup
- After creating the project file, add a link to it in the ideas list (e.g. `→ [project](projects/work-5-slug.md)`)

## Kicking off a project
- `/proj-kickoff <ID>` (e.g. `/proj-kickoff WORK-3`) — start a project from an idea
- Asks user for format (md or org) and problem statement / solution if not provided
- Spawns project-lead → creates work breakdown in `~/.claude/ideas/projects/`
- User reviews and approves before implementation begins
- Ideas with `CHORE` or `BUG` in the ID (e.g. CHORE-1, BUG-3) don't require a full project doc — just execute directly

## Implementation
On user approval:
1. Project-lead spawns worker agents per task (parallel where deps allow)
2. Each worker uses its own worktree under `~/.claude/worktrees/`
3. Workers update the project file as tasks complete
4. Project-lead reports back when all tasks are done
5. User owns commits and final sign-off
